7. On "Open Source"

RigelMCMod publishes its full source code, welcomes forks and contributions, and can be
freely used and modified for non-commercial purposes - it is developed and presented as an
open-source project in that everyday sense. It is worth being precise, though: because of
the source-only-redistribution requirement (Section 3) and the reserved commercial rights
(Section 4), this License does not meet the OSI Open Source Definition or the FSF Free
Software Definition, both of which require unrestricted redistribution in any form and no
discrimination against fields of endeavor, commercial use included. If that formal
distinction matters for your use case, treat RigelMCMod as source-available, not
OSI-certified open source.

